module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Defs
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.FiniteDimensional.Defs
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.TensorProduct.Basic

/-!
# Testing geometric integrality on finite field extensions

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0FWF, `algebra-lemma-characterize-geometrically-integral`,
implication (2) to (1), and `algebra-definition-geometrically-integral`
(blueprint: lem-per-geometric-integrality-finite-field-test).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v w

open TensorProduct

namespace Algebra

/-- A commutative algebra whose tensor product with every finite field extension is a
domain has domain tensor product with every field extension. There is no finite-generation
or Noetherian assumption on the algebra. The test-field universe contains the universes
of the base field, the algebra, and the arbitrary extension; finite extensions admit
models in this universe.

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0FWF, `algebra-lemma-characterize-geometrically-integral`,
implication (2) to (1)
(blueprint: lem-per-geometric-integrality-finite-field-test).

Proof (the node's agent-written coefficient-specialization proof; Stacks proves Tag 0FWF
instead through geometric reducedness and geometric irreducibility):

1. Take the degree-one extension `k/k` in the hypothesis. The mutually inverse maps
   `S ⊗[k] k → S`, `s ⊗ c ↦ c • s`, and `S → S ⊗[k] k`, `s ↦ s ⊗ 1`, show that `S` is a
   domain, hence `1 ≠ 0` in `S`. Fix an arbitrary extension `K/k`. By
   [lem-weil-pbc-vector-space-basis-through-vector] (`Module.Basis.extend`), choose a
   `k`-basis `(e_i)_{i ∈ I}` of `S` containing `1`.
2. For each commutative `k`-algebra `T`, let `λ_i : S → k` be the coefficient of `e_i`.
   Every tensor in `S ⊗[k] T` has a unique finite expansion `∑ e_i ⊗ t_i`: expand each
   first factor in the basis; uniqueness follows by applying the `k`-linear maps
   `s ⊗ t ↦ λ_i(s) t`, defined by the balanced tensor relations. For a `k`-algebra map
   `f : T → T'`, the map `1 ⊗ f` therefore sends the coefficients `(t_i)` to `(f t_i)` and
   is injective whenever `f` is. It is multiplicative on pure tensors, by
   `(s ⊗ t)(s' ⊗ u) = s s' ⊗ t u`, hence everywhere. In particular `S ⊗[k] K` is nonzero:
   the coefficient of the basis vector `1` in `1 ⊗ 1` is `1 ≠ 0` in `K`.
3. Suppose `x y = 0` with `x, y ∈ S ⊗[k] K` nonzero. Write their coefficient families
   `(a_i)` and `(b_i)` by Step 2 and choose `a_{i₀} ≠ 0`, `b_{j₀} ≠ 0`. Let `A ⊆ K` be the
   `k`-subalgebra generated by these finitely many coefficients and by `a_{i₀}⁻¹`,
   `b_{j₀}⁻¹`; it is a nonzero finitely generated `k`-algebra. The same finite sums define
   `x_A, y_A ∈ S ⊗[k] A` mapping to `x, y`; Step 2 for `A ↪ K` gives `x_A y_A = 0`.
4. By [lem-weil-common-extension-fields-maximal-ideal] choose a maximal ideal `m` of the
   nontrivial ring `A`; by [lem-weil-common-extension-fields-quotient-field] `L = A/m` is a
   field. The composite `k → A → L` makes `L` a field extension of `k`
   [def-pc-field-extension], generated as a `k`-algebra by the images of the generators of
   `A`. By [lem-weil-pbc-zariski-lemma] (`finite_of_finite_type_of_isJacobsonRing`), `L/k`
   is finite; transport it along a `k`-algebra equivalence into `Type (max u v w)`.
5. Let `x̄, ȳ` be the images of `x_A, y_A` in `S ⊗[k] L`. Their product is zero by the
   multiplicativity of Step 2. Their selected coordinates are the residues of `a_{i₀}` and
   `b_{j₀}`, which are units of `A` (their inverses were adjoined), hence nonzero in the
   field `L`. By the coordinate uniqueness of Step 2, `x̄ ≠ 0` and `ȳ ≠ 0`, contradicting
   `hfinite L`. Hence `S ⊗[k] K` has no zero divisors, and it is nonzero by Step 2, so it
   is a domain. -/
theorem isDomain_tensorProduct_of_finite_field_extensions
    (k : Type u) (S : Type v) [Field k] [CommRing S] [Algebra k S]
    (hfinite : ∀ (L : Type (max u v w)) [Field L] [Algebra k L]
      [FiniteDimensional k L], IsDomain (S ⊗[k] L))
    (K : Type w) [Field K] [Algebra k K] :
    IsDomain (S ⊗[k] K) := by
  sorry

end Algebra
